---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../data/tools';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: absoluteUrl('/') },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Methodology', item: absoluteUrl('/methodology/') },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology & Correction Log | YardCalc"
  description="How YardCalc formulas, bag yields, and citations are checked. AI disclosure, what the tools leave out, and a public correction log."
  canonicalPath="/methodology/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Methodology' }]} />
  <article class="container prose panel" style="margin:1rem auto 3rem;max-width:800px">
    <h1>Methodology &amp; editorial policy</h1>
    <p class="answer-first">
      {siteConfig.name} tools are planning estimators. I ({siteConfig.author.name}) document the formulas in
      code with unit tests, cite manufacturer and extension sources on each page, and only change “last
      reviewed” dates when the formula, yields, sources, or explanatory text actually change.
    </p>

    <h2>How numbers are produced</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pure TypeScript functions in <code>src/lib/calculators/</code> with Vitest coverage</li>
      <li>
        US bag yields aligned to common QUIKRETE / Sakrete published coverage (about 0.30 / 0.45 / 0.60 cu ft
        for 40 / 60 / 80 lb mixes)—always verify the bag you buy
      </li>
      <li>Waste factors disclosed in the UI and article text</li>
      <li>Unit conversions centralized in <code>src/lib/units.ts</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources policy</h2>
    <p>
      Prefer primary sources: manufacturer calculators and data sheets, American Cement Association (formerly
      Portland Cement Association) materials, ACI residential concrete guidance, and university extension
      pages for mulch depth and lawn fertility. Each tool’s Sources list states what the URL is used for and
      the date I checked it. I do not invent credentials or anonymous “editorial team” bylines.
    </p>

    <h2>What the calculators leave out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Structural design (rebar, thickness for loads, frost heave engineering)</li>
      <li>Local code minimums and permit requirements</li>
      <li>Site-specific soil, drainage, and access constraints</li>
      <li>Specialty mix yields that differ from standard bag charts</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>AI / automation disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      Drafts may be written with AI assistance; every formula, rate, and reference is checked by{' '}
      {siteConfig.author.name} against the sources listed on each page before publish.
    </p>

    <h2>Date policy</h2>
    <p>
      <code>datePublished</code> is the first release. <code>dateModified</code> / “Last reviewed” change only
      when content or math changes—not to fake freshness. Site-wide stamp: {siteConfig.contentUpdated}.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changelog">Correction log &amp; changelog</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Date</th>
            <th>Change</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>2026-10-20</td>
            <td>
              Mobile SEO / E-E-A-T pass: named maintainer ({siteConfig.author.name}), TrustBlock sources,
              Consent Mode v2 analytics, hub and guide expansions, favicon/OG set, embed snippets.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>2026-10-19</td>
            <td>Initial public release of calculator suite and guides.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Report errors via <a href="/contact/">contact</a> · Maintainer: <a href="/about/#maintainer">{siteConfig.author.name}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
